# d21 — Middleware

Eight problems on the layer between the socket and your handler, drawn from
lessons **09_express_fundamentals** and **10_middleware_validation**. Lesson 10
gave you one logger, one validator, one error handler and one rate limiter; this
set is the family they belong to — combinators, guards, the error stack, and the
two middlewares that have to wrap the response object to do their job.

Nothing here waits. Anything time-shaped takes an injected `now`, and the
concurrency problem is graded by holding handlers open with deferreds the gate
releases by hand, so "two at once" is a count and not a race.

## Problem 1 — how long did that take

Edit `ex1/solution.ts`. `timing({ now })` sets `x-response-time` to the
milliseconds elapsed on the injected clock. The header has to be on the response
before it goes out, which rules out the obvious hook.

```
uv run dojo test node d21_middleware ex1
```

## Problem 2 — the same middleware, but not everywhere

Edit `ex2/solution.ts`. `only(predicate, middleware)` runs the wrapped
middleware when `predicate(req)` is true and skips it otherwise — without
stopping the request. A `next(error)` from inside must reach the error handler
unchanged.

```
uv run dojo test node d21_middleware ex2
```

## Problem 3 — a stack wearing one middleware's coat

Edit `ex3/solution.ts`. `compose(...middlewares)` folds them into one: in order,
each waiting for the last to call `next()`; one that responds stops the rest;
a `next(error)` skips the remainder **and** the routes below; and `compose()`
with nothing is a middleware that only calls `next()`.

```
uv run dojo test node d21_middleware ex3
```

## Problem 4 — two error handlers, and one that knows when to give up

Edit `ex4/solution.ts`. `createApp({ log })` has a first error handler that
answers what it recognises with 422 and passes everything else on with
`next(error)`, an ordinary middleware between the two, and the exported
`finalErrorHandler` at the bottom: 500, nothing revealing, one log line — and
`next(error)` instead when `res.headersSent` is already true.

```
uv run dojo test node d21_middleware ex4
```

## Problem 5 — bodies at the edge

Edit `ex5/solution.ts`. `createApp()` configures `express.json({ limit, type })`
and translates every refusal into one JSON error shape: 413
`payload_too_large`, 400 `invalid_json`, 415 `unsupported_media_type`, 400
`empty_body`. Two of those four the parser raises for you; two it does not.

```
uv run dojo test node d21_middleware ex5
```

## Problem 6 — who you are, and whether that is enough

Edit `ex6/solution.ts`. `bearerAuth({ verify })` reads `Authorization: Bearer`,
calls `verify`, and attaches the principal — 401 with `WWW-Authenticate` for no
token, the wrong scheme, or a token `verify` rejects. `requireRole(role)` then
answers 403, with no challenge, for a principal who is who they say and still
may not.

```
uv run dojo test node d21_middleware ex6
```

## Problem 7 — how many requests are allowed inside

Edit `ex7/solution.ts`. `concurrencyLimit({ max, queue })` admits `max` at a
time, parks up to `queue` more, and refuses the rest with `503 { error:
"too_busy" }` and `Retry-After`. A request that failed gives its slot back like
any other.

```
uv run dojo test node d21_middleware ex7
```

## Problem 8 — answering without running the handler

Edit `ex8/solution.ts`. `responseCache({ ttl, now })` stores successful `GET`
responses per method and url and replays them with `x-cache: HIT` (fresh is
`MISS`). Never a non-2xx, never when the request says `cache-control: no-store`,
expiring on the injected clock, and dropping everything stored for a path when a
write arrives at it.

```
uv run dojo test node d21_middleware ex8
```

## The gate

This is the entire gate, `gate.test.ts`:

```ts
// Gate for node/d21_middleware. Quoted verbatim in README.md.
//
// Everything time-shaped runs on a clock the gate owns, and everything
// concurrency-shaped is held open by a deferred the gate releases by hand - so
// no test sleeps, and every claim about "how many at once" is a count rather
// than a race. supertest gives each app an ephemeral port and takes it away
// again, so nothing binds a fixed one.
import express, { type Express, type NextFunction, type Request, type Response } from "express";
import request from "supertest";
import { describe, expect, test, vi } from "vitest";

import { timing } from "./ex1/solution";
import { only } from "./ex2/solution";
import { compose } from "./ex3/solution";
import { createApp as createErrorApp } from "./ex4/solution";
import { finalErrorHandler } from "./ex4/solution";
import { createApp as createBodyApp } from "./ex5/solution";
import { bearerAuth, requireRole } from "./ex6/solution";
import { concurrencyLimit } from "./ex7/solution";
import { responseCache } from "./ex8/solution";

/** Hand a promise back plus the buttons that settle it. */
function deferred<T = void>() {
  let resolve!: (value: T) => void;
  let reject!: (reason: unknown) => void;
  const promise = new Promise<T>((res, rej) => {
    resolve = res;
    reject = rej;
  });
  return { promise, resolve, reject };
}

/** Turn the event loop over until `check()` holds, or fail loudly. */
async function until(check: () => boolean, what: string): Promise<void> {
  for (let tick = 0; tick < 2_000; tick += 1) {
    if (check()) return;
    await new Promise<void>((resolve) => setImmediate(resolve));
  }
  throw new Error(`the gate gave up waiting for: ${what}`);
}

describe("d21_middleware", () => {
  test("ex1 - timing() reports the clock it was given, on the response", async () => {
    let clock = 1_000;
    const app = express();
    app.use(timing({ now: () => clock }));
    app.get("/fast", (_req, res) => {
      clock += 7;
      res.json({ status: "ok" });
    });
    app.get("/slow", async (_req, res) => {
      clock += 250;
      res.json({ status: "ok" });
    });

    const fast = await request(app).get("/fast");
    expect(fast.status, "the middleware must not change the response itself").toBe(200);
    expect(fast.body, "or its body").toEqual({ status: "ok" });
    expect(
      fast.headers["x-response-time"],
      "the elapsed time on the injected clock, in milliseconds, as a plain " +
        "number - a real Date.now() here would make this test flaky forever",
    ).toBe("7");

    clock = 5_000;
    const slow = await request(app).get("/slow");
    expect(
      slow.headers["x-response-time"],
      "and it measures each request separately: the start time belongs to this " +
        "request, not to the middleware",
    ).toBe("250");

    const missing = await request(app).get("/nowhere");
    expect(missing.status, "an unmatched path is still a 404").toBe(404);
    expect(
      missing.headers["x-response-time"],
      "which the middleware still timed - it is registered above the routes, " +
        "so it sees requests no route answered",
    ).toBeDefined();
  }, 30_000);

  test("ex2 - only() runs the middleware where it belongs and gets out of the way elsewhere", async () => {
    const stamped: string[] = [];
    const stamp = (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
      stamped.push(req.path);
      res.setHeader("x-stamped", "yes");
      next();
    };

    const app = express();
    app.use(only((req) => !req.path.startsWith("/public"), stamp));
    app.get("/private", (_req, res) => res.json({ area: "private" }));
    app.get("/public/logo", (_req, res) => res.json({ area: "public" }));

    const guarded = await request(app).get("/private");
    expect(
      guarded.headers["x-stamped"],
      "the predicate said yes, so the wrapped middleware ran",
    ).toBe("yes");

    const skipped = await request(app).get("/public/logo");
    expect(skipped.status, "the request still reaches its route").toBe(200);
    expect(skipped.body, "and is answered normally").toEqual({ area: "public" });
    expect(
      skipped.headers["x-stamped"],
      "but the wrapped middleware never ran. Skipping means calling next() " +
        "yourself, not returning - a middleware that returns without calling " +
        "next() hangs the request until the client times out",
    ).toBeUndefined();

    expect(stamped, "one path in, one path out").toEqual(["/private"]);

    const failing = (_req: Request, _res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
      next(new Error("the wrapped one exploded"));
    };
    const second = express();
    second.use(only(() => true, failing));
    second.get("/anything", (_req, res) => res.json({ reached: true }));
    second.use((error: Error, _req: Request, res: Response, _next: NextFunction) => {
      res.status(500).json({ error: error.message });
    });

    const propagated = await request(second).get("/anything");
    expect(
      propagated.body,
      "and a next(error) from inside the wrapped middleware has to reach the " +
        "error handler unchanged - the wrapper is a pass-through, not a catch",
    ).toEqual({ error: "the wrapped one exploded" });
